# Kinetum SDK @KINETUM_VERSION@

Development kit for building custom Kinetum modules.

## Installation

The versioned release page supplies a complete command for each architecture.
The public headers are common to both architectures; the native installer in
each archive determines its target. In this x86-64 example, `RELEASE_URL`
denotes the versioned HTTPS asset directory from that page:

```bash
set -o pipefail
curl -fsSL "$RELEASE_URL/kinetum-sdk-@KINETUM_VERSION@-x86_64.install.sh" | sudo /bin/sh
```

For AArch64, choose `kinetum-sdk-@KINETUM_VERSION@-aarch64.install.sh`.
Each entry verifies its selected architecture and archive before installation.
Ubuntu 24.04 and the native installer's system-library prerequisites are
required. Online setup installs those libraries through APT.

The public installer's CPU requirements are the Core i7/Nehalem instruction-set
baseline on x86-64 and Armv8-A with CRC on AArch64. A locally built native
installer may require additional features of its build machine. SDK headers
remain common, and the exported CMake/pkg-config interfaces leave CPU flags
to the module author's build.

The same `.install.sh` is produced for a locally built package. To install
local files, including on an offline host, place it beside its matching
`.tar.gz`. With the system prerequisites already installed, run from that
directory on x86-64:

```bash
sudo /bin/sh ./kinetum-sdk-@KINETUM_VERSION@-x86_64.install.sh --offline-directory "$PWD"
```

This mode skips downloads and APT installation. An installer produced without
a publication URL requires `--offline-directory`; it never invents an address
or chooses a local archive implicitly.

The SDK installs under `/opt/kinetum/sdk`; `--prefix /absolute/path` selects
another protected prefix. Only `sdk/` is replaced, with prior bytes retained
until installed verification succeeds. A failed replacement restores prior
entries; a restoration failure reports both causes and retains recovery state.
Runtime and build-dependency peers remain untouched. With `--offline-directory`,
installed prerequisites, and a user-owned prefix, SDK installation can run
without root.

The SDK contains headers, examples, build integration, and applicable legal
material. The documentation website is built and published separately.
The release channel authenticates delivery; checksums provide integrity
under that channel.

## Discovery

```bash
export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="/opt/kinetum/sdk/lib/pkgconfig${PKG_CONFIG_PATH:+:${PKG_CONFIG_PATH}}"
pkg-config --cflags --libs kinetum
```

For C++20 projects:

```cmake
find_package(Kinetum CONFIG REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my_module PRIVATE Kinetum::SDK)
```

Point CMake at the installed package domain when configuring:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/kinetum/sdk
```

Examples are installed under `sdk/examples/`. The source repository's `docs/`
index and the separately published website own the guides and API reference;
they are not installed into the SDK. Package-level legal material remains at
the SDK root.
